id: decisions/020-break-grammar-alias-addresses
title: "Old names: break the grammar, alias the address"
kind: decision-frame
record_shape: header
status: draft
authored_by: agent-on-behalf
authored_via: "Claude Code session on Dave's behalf, 2026-09-12; drafted from the JUDGMENTS.md reversal axis 'legacy-handling'; recommendation is an attributed inference and ratifies nothing"
version: "1.0.0"
updated: 2026-09-12
template_ref: templates/decision.yawn
principal_ref: principal:dave

axis: legacy-handling
axis_gloss: "whether the old name, route or address keeps working alongside the new one, or is broken so only the new one remains"
poles:
  a: hard-break
  b: retain-alias
split: leaning
draft_status: full
leverage: 13.7
leverage_formula: "judgments_resolved(13) x split_weight(0.7) x authored_conflict_bonus(1.5)"

question: >
  When a name changes, does the old name stop working, as .ion and .yon did, or
  keep working as a marked alias, as core-loop.yawn and /declaration-of-agency
  do?

why_it_matters: >
  The repo hard-broke the old extension, the old schema layer and the old brand,
  but kept old field names, old routes, the old repository slug and a superseded
  loop file alive, and yawn.yawn registers that superseded file twice. Once
  answered, a bot that meets an old name knows whether to resolve it or refuse
  it, and a child Yawn knows which of its parent's names it may still use.

state_snapshot:
  description: >
    3 judgments sit at hard-break (3 authored) and 10 at retain-alias (8
    authored). The repository asserts both directions on this axis.
  attributed_to: JUDGMENTS.md
  epistemic_status: observed
  source_refs:
    - JUDGMENTS.md
    - TAXONOMY.md

lacuna:
  kind: unselected_choice
  description: >
    Whether aliases expire (declaration-of-agency/node.yawn has no sunset), and
    whether the repository slug is a permanent alias or only waiting on
    infrastructure authority.

options:
  - name: A
    description: >
      rule: Old names stop working. Retired extensions, routes, field names, slugs
      and files are removed from the active tree; their content is preserved only
      through Git history and one migration receipt.
    cost: "Remove declaration-of-agency/node.yawn redirect, core-loop.yawn, the legacy compatibility examples and the schemas/node.yawn compatibility clause; rename the repository slug."
    risk: "Eight authored judgments chose aliases for stated reasons (preserve first route, preserve lineage, exercise edge normalization); breaking them loses what they were kept for."
    reversibility: false
    proof_needed: "Extend scripts/validate-canonical-extension.mjs to reject any file with status: superseded or a redirects_to key."
    evidence_for:
      - "J0293"
      - "J0470"
      - "J0896"
  - name: B
    description: >
      rule: Old names keep working. Every retired name remains readable, marked
      superseded or redirects_to, and registered in yawn.yawn; nothing is
      hard-broken.
    cost: "Reverses the extension migration's fail-closed rollback clause and the no-second-schema-layer choice in CHANGELOG.md."
    risk: "Aliases accumulate without expiry; the double registration in yawn.yawn is the first symptom."
    reversibility: true
    proof_needed: "A test that every superseded file is registered in yawn.yawn."
    evidence_for:
      - "J0298"
      - "J0379"
      - "J0605"
      - "J0755"
      - "J0756"
      - "J0799"
  - name: C
    description: >
      rule: The record grammar breaks hard: a retired extension, schema layer or
      brand identity is never restored, and rollback reads old content only at the
      old commit. Addresses and fields alias: a retired route, slug, field name or
      file stays readable, carries status: superseded or redirects_to with a reason,
      and is registered exactly once in yawn.yawn. An alias with no reason or no
      registration is a defect.
    cost: "One rule added to migrations/node.yawn; yawn.yawn deduplicated so core-loop.yawn is registered once."
    risk: "The grammar/address line is contested for things like schema field names, which are both."
    reversibility: true
    proof_needed: "scripts/validate-canonical-extension.mjs already fails closed on grammar aliases; add a test in tests/ that every file with status: superseded or a redirects_to key is registered exactly once in yawn.yawn and carries a reason."
    evidence_for:
      - "J0293"
      - "J0896"
      - "J0298"
      - "J0605"
      - "J0755"
      - "J0756"

recommendation:
  option_ref: C
  confidence: 0.8
  epistemic_status: inferred
  authority: none
  reasoning: >
    Both sides are authored (3 and 8) and both have proof-attached members (J0293
    through validate-canonical-extension; J0605 through the promotion receipt), so
    this is not a rule with an exception but two rules for two classes. The
    pattern is already consistent: everything on the hard-break side is grammar or
    identity (extension, schema layer, brand); everything on the alias side is an
    address or a field (route, slug, field names, a superseded file). The only
    untidy item is J0799, the superseded loop registered twice with no expiry,
    which is what the once-and-with-a-reason clause catches. C writes the existing
    pattern down.

target_condition:
  mode: decide
  proposed_by: agent-on-behalf
  affected_principals:
    - principal:dave
  ratification_status: proposed
  sufficient_when:
    - "migrations/node.yawn or core/canonical-extension.yawn states the two classes: grammar breaks, addresses alias."
    - "yawn.yawn registers core-loop.yawn once, with the reason from its supersession_reason."
    - "A test in tests/ fails when a superseded or redirecting file is unregistered or registered twice, and passes."

choice:
  selected_by: ""
  option_ref: ""
  authority_ref: ""

proof:
  covers:
    - target_condition
    - options
  falsifier: >
    A schema field is renamed with no compatibility shim while a new route is
    broken with no redirect, that is, the classes are applied backwards and
    nothing notices.
  status: proposed

resolves:
  - "J0293"
  - "J0470"
  - "J0896"
  - "J0298"
  - "J0379"
  - "J0605"
  - "J0755"
  - "J0756"
  - "J0799"

boundary:
  - "confidence cannot ratify an objective; this record proposes and Dave decides"
  - "the REVEALED judgments cited here describe what artifacts do, not what Dave chose"

source:
  template: templates/decision.yawn
  axis: TAXONOMY.md
  inventory: JUDGMENTS.md
